        <div id="discounting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Exercise Bike Discounting Actually Looks Like</h2>
<p>
            Exercise bikes do get genuinely cheaper in late November. They also get promoted in ways
            that make an everyday price look like a once-a-year event. Four patterns came up
            repeatedly while we were checking the bikes on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The list price is an anchor, not a price.</strong> Horizon's 5.0R recumbent had a
            list price of $999 and a selling price of $649 on Horizon's own store on 19 October. That
            is a 35% "saving" in the middle of October, with no holiday in sight. BarBend, which reviewed it, says it is routinely
            on sale. If the 5.0R appears at $649 on Black Friday with a "35% off" banner, nothing has
            happened: that is its normal price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Prices move week to week, not just in November.</strong> Schwinn had the IC4 at
            $799 on 16 October and $899 three days later. A bike that swings by $100 within a week
            can easily be "reduced" to a price it already reached in October. The useful number is
            the lowest price in the last month or two, not the list price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Past lows can be far below today's prices, and are not a promise.</strong> Deal
            posts on Slickdeals record the IC4 at $499.99 on Amazon in December 2022, bundled with a
            year of Schwinn's JRNY membership, and at $499.99 <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>at Best Buy</a> for paid members in July
            2023. Hip2Save recorded the AirDyne AD7 at $899.99 on Amazon in April 2022. Those prices
            are three or four years old; we have no evidence they will return this year, and nobody
            can honestly tell you they will. What they do show is that the list prices quoted against
            today's sales have been the same, or close, for years.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cheap bikes discount in coupons, expensive ones quietly or not at all.</strong>
            Amazon-first brands such as YOSUDA and Marcy price in tens of dollars, and their "deals"
            are often clip-on coupons or colour-by-colour price differences that come and go all
            year; BarBend saw Marcy's folding bike at $179.29 in black and $140 in white on Amazon at
            the same time. At the other end, Keiser's own store says it runs "specials every now and
            again" and asks buyers to phone for current offers. For a studio bike, ask the maker
            directly before assuming the Amazon price is the best one available.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/treadmill-black-friday-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday method page</a>
            covers the general rules — record prices before the sale, compare the delivered price at
            more than one retailer, and ignore the "was" price — so we will not repeat them here.
            The rest of this page is about what is particular to bikes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Subscription Bundles Counted as Savings</h2>
<p>
            The most common way an exercise bike deal is inflated is by counting a membership as
            money off. A year of an app's subscription is added to the box, its full retail value is
            added to the headline saving, and a bike reduced by $100 becomes a bike "worth" several
            hundred dollars more than you pay.
          </p>
          <p>
            The December 2022 IC4 deal above is a fair example of a bundle that was genuinely good,
            because the bike itself was heavily reduced and the membership was extra. The test is the
            same one we apply to treadmills: <strong>take the membership out and look at the bike
            price on its own</strong>. If that is a real reduction on what it sold for in October,
            the membership is a bonus. If the saving only looks large once the membership's retail
            value is added, the deal is on the app, not the bike.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things decide what an included membership is worth to you:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Would you have paid for it anyway?</strong> A year of an app you were going
            to use is worth what you would have paid. A year of one you were not is worth nothing,
            however it is priced on the banner.</li>
            <li><strong>What does it renew into?</strong> Many included periods renew automatically
            at the full rate. Note the end date when you activate it.</li>
            <li><strong>Does the bike need it?</strong> On an open bike like the IC4 the app is
            optional and the bike works fully on its own console. On a closed bike, the membership
            is part of the cost of ownership, and an included period only delays when you start
            paying.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We do not quote subscription prices on this page, because the big platforms have changed
            them in the past year and we could not confirm current figures on the day. Check the
            platform's own pricing page before you count a membership as part of a deal. Our
            <a href="/ifit-vs-peloton/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT vs Peloton comparison</a>
            explains how the two largest ecosystems work and what you lose if you stop paying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Magnetic vs Friction Resistance: Do Not Let a Discount Decide</h2>
<p>
            The biggest price gaps between similar-looking spin bikes usually come down to how the
            resistance works, and a Black Friday discount can make the cheaper kind look like the
            obvious buy. It is worth knowing what you give up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Friction resistance</strong> presses a felt or leather pad against the flywheel,
            controlled by a knob. It is cheap to build and gives continuous, stepless adjustment.
            The downsides are wear and noise: the pad wears and needs replacing, and owners of
            friction bikes commonly report squeaks developing over months. The YOSUDA on this page
            and Schwinn's IC3 both use it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Magnetic resistance</strong> moves magnets closer to or further from the
            flywheel without touching it. Nothing rubs, so there is nothing to wear out and very
            little noise, and on bikes with electronic control it allows numbered levels — 100 on
            the IC4 and the Horizon 5.0R, eight on the Marcy folding bike. It costs more to build,
            which is why most bikes under about $300 use friction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Fan resistance</strong>, on air bikes like the AirDyne AD7, is different again:
            the harder you pedal, the more air the fan moves and the harder it gets. There are no
            levels to set. It suits hard intervals and is the loudest of the three; our
            <a href="/best-air-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">air bike guide</a>
            explains the trade-offs.
          </p>
          <p>
            How this plays out in a sale: at full price, the friction YOSUDA and a magnetic bike like
            the IC4 are hundreds of dollars apart, and the choice is easy for a tight budget. If a
            Black Friday cut brings a magnetic bike within $100 or so of a friction one you were
            considering, the magnetic bike is usually the better long-term buy for a shared home or
            an upstairs room. If the gap stays wide, a friction bike is a perfectly sound way to
            start, provided you accept the occasional pad replacement. Do not read too much into
            flywheel weight in either case: Keiser's studio bike uses a comparatively light rear
            flywheel and is one of the smoothest-riding bikes made.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Bluetooth, Zwift and Locked Ecosystems</h2>
<p>
            "Works with <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Zwift</a>" and "compatible with Peloton" appear on a great many Black Friday
            listings, and they mean less than they suggest. Before a discount persuades you, check
            three things.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which apps the maker actually lists.</strong> Bikes broadcast cadence and
            estimated power over Bluetooth, and the industry standard for that is called FTMS.
            Schwinn is candid that the IC4 has <em>partial</em> FTMS, which means it works with the
            specific apps Schwinn names — JRNY, Peloton, Zwift, Kinomap and Explore the World — rather
            than anything that speaks the standard. Horizon lists Bluetooth FTMS on the 5.0R and
            names Peloton, Zwift and Kinomap. Keiser's M Connect display sends data to apps over
            Bluetooth. The AirDyne AD7, the YOSUDA and the Marcy do not connect to apps at all. If
            one app matters to you, find it on the maker's list rather than the listing title.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whether the app can control the bike.</strong> On the IC4 and similar bikes you
            set resistance by hand, so Zwift's virtual hills cannot change it for you, and the power
            figure is calculated from cadence and resistance rather than measured. That is fine for
            following along; it is not a power meter. If accurate, app-controlled resistance is the
            point, look at
            <a href="/best-smart-bike-trainers/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart bike trainers</a>
            instead, which get their own November discounts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Whether the bike is locked to one platform.</strong> Peloton, Echelon and
            iFIT-based bikes are built around their own subscription. You get a large screen and
            classes that can adjust the bike, but some or all of that depends on keeping the
            membership, and OutdoorGearLab reports that Echelon bikes now work only when connected to
            Echelon's servers. A deep discount on a closed bike can still be a good deal, but only
            once you have added the membership you will need for as long as you expect to ride it.
            An open bike at a smaller discount often costs less over three years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="peloton" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Peloton on Black Friday: The Recalls and What to Check</h2>
<p>
            Many people searching for Black Friday bike deals are really looking for a cheap Peloton.
            We have not listed one, for two reasons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The two original bikes have both been recalled.</strong> In May 2023 Peloton
            recalled about 2.2 million original Peloton Bikes, model PL01, because the seat post
            could break during use. In November 2025 it recalled about 833,000 Original Series Bike+
            units in the US, model PL02 with serial numbers beginning with "T", for the same hazard.
            We confirmed both on the CPSC's database on 28 October 2026. The remedy in each case is a
            free replacement seat post that owners fit themselves. Both bikes were sold on Amazon as
            well as by Peloton, and both turn up second-hand and through marketplace sellers — which
            is exactly where a "Black Friday" price on an old Peloton is likely to appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you are offered an original Peloton Bike or Bike+</strong>, find the model
            number on the inside of the front fork and the serial number before you pay. A PL01, or
            a PL02 with a "T" serial, needs the replacement seat post fitted before anyone rides it,
            and the seller should be able to show that it has been. If they cannot, walk away or
            price in the time to arrange the fix with Peloton.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Peloton's current bikes are a different case.</strong> The Cross Training Bike and
            Cross Training Bike+ are not part of either recall. When we checked for our exercise bike
            roundup on 19 October we could not confirm a current Amazon listing for them, could not
            get Peloton and an independent source to agree on key figures, and Peloton says a
            membership is required to use its content. That keeps them off a list built on open
            bikes with checkable figures. If what you want is Peloton's classes, the cheaper route is
            usually the Peloton app on an open bike such as the IC4; our
            <a href="/best-peloton-alternatives/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton alternatives guide</a>
            goes through the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="delivery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Parcel or Freight, and the Box You Have to Carry</h2>
<p>
            Bikes sit across the line between parcel and freight, and Black Friday delivery terms
            are where a cheap price can stop being cheap. The bike's own weight is a rough guide to
            what will arrive; the box is always heavier.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Folding and budget bikes</strong> — the Marcy at about 37 lb by reviewers'
            figures, the YOSUDA at 68.8 lb as measured by OutdoorGearLab — normally come as ordinary
            parcels left at the door. One person can usually manage them.</li>
            <li><strong>Mid-range spin and recumbent bikes</strong> are heavier: <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>BowFlex</a> gives 113.6 lb
            for the IC4's twin, the C6, including its dumbbells, and Horizon gives 123 lb for the
            5.0R. These may come by parcel carrier or by freight depending on the retailer, and either
            way you will want two people to move the box.</li>
            <li><strong>Air and studio bikes</strong> — 113 lb for the AD7 on Schwinn's figures, 92
            lb for the Keiser M3i on Keiser's page (other sources differ) — are heavy, awkward boxes.
            Check whether delivery is to the door, the kerb or a room of your choice.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Three checks before you order in November. First, read the delivery line on the listing,
            not the product title: "free delivery" on a heavy item can mean kerbside. Second, check
            whether assembly is offered and what it costs, because a $100 discount can be swallowed
            by a $150 assembly add-on you did not plan for. Third, look at the return terms for a
            bike of that weight — a return usually means partly dismantling it, repacking it and
            booking a collection — and keep the box until you are sure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Holiday deliveries can also be slower. If the bike is a gift, order early in the sale window
            rather than on <a href="/cyber-monday-treadmill-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cyber Monday</a>, and confirm the delivery estimate on the order page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. When the 2026 Deals Start</h2>
<p>
            None of the retailers on this page had announced Black Friday 2026 dates or prices when we
            wrote this on 28 October. The pattern in recent years has been consistent enough to plan
            around:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Now to mid-November.</strong> Maker stores such as Schwinn, BowFlex and
            Horizon run rolling promotions all autumn, as the IC4's $799-to-$899 move in October
            shows. Use these weeks to record prices for the bikes you are considering, at Amazon and
            the maker's store.</li>
            <li><strong>The week before Thanksgiving.</strong> Amazon's Black Friday event has
            typically opened about a week before Thanksgiving, which this year would mean around
            the week of 16 November. We do not know Amazon's 2026 dates.</li>
            <li><strong>Thanksgiving to Cyber Monday</strong> — Thursday 26 November to Monday
            30 November. The main window, when maker stores usually match or join in. Prices can
            change during the weekend, and a lower price after you order is not refunded
            automatically, so check whether the retailer offers price adjustments and look at the
            listing again within its return window.</li>
            <li><strong>December and January.</strong> Exercise bikes are a New Year purchase for many
            people, and some promotions run on into January. If nothing reaches your target price in
            November, waiting is a reasonable choice, not a lost opportunity.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For the rest of the home gym, our
            <a href="/black-friday-home-gym-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday home gym deals</a>
            page covers strength and cardio equipment, and if you are torn between a bike and a
            cross-trainer, see
            <a href="/black-friday-elliptical-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday elliptical deals</a>.
            Treadmills are covered in our
            <a href="/black-friday-treadmill-deals-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Black Friday treadmill deals for 2026</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Black Friday exercise bike deals worth waiting for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if you already know which bike you want. Late November is one of the stronger sale periods for home cardio equipment. But many advertised savings are measured against list prices the bikes rarely sell at, so compare the Black Friday price with what the bike sold for in October, not with the "was" price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good Black Friday price for a Schwinn IC4?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In our view, $749 or less. Schwinn had it at $799 on 16 October 2026 and $899 on 19 October, against a $999 list price, so $799 is an ordinary sale price rather than a Black Friday one. It sold for $499.99 in 2022 and 2023 promotions, but those are old prices, not a forecast.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a Peloton on Black Friday?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Be careful with the original models. The Peloton Bike (PL01) was recalled in May 2023 and the original Bike+ (PL02, serials starting with T) in November 2025, both because the seat post can break. If you are offered one, confirm the replacement seat post has been fitted. Peloton's bikes also need a membership for their content, so price that in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a free year of app membership part of the discount?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you would have paid for that app anyway. Take the membership out and judge the bike price on its own against what it sold for recently. Check what the included period renews into, and whether the bike needs the membership to work properly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Magnetic or friction resistance: which should I buy in a sale?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Magnetic, if the sale brings it within about $100 of a friction bike you were considering. Magnetic resistance is quieter and has no pad to wear out. Friction bikes are cheaper and work well, but the pad needs replacing over time and can become noisy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When do Amazon exercise bike deals start for Black Friday 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Amazon had not announced its 2026 dates when we checked on 28 October. Its Black Friday event has typically started about a week before Thanksgiving, which this year falls on 26 November, and runs through Cyber Monday on 30 November. Maker stores run their own sales through the autumn.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying an Exercise Bike on Black Friday</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from judging the deal before judging the bike, which is easy to do when a timer is counting down.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the saving from the list price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Exercise bike list prices are often prices nobody pays. The Horizon 5.0R sold at $649 against a $999 list in October, so "35% off" in November would be its normal price. Record what your shortlist sells for in late October and early November and judge any deal against that.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting the membership as money off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A bundled year of an app can make a small reduction look large. Take it out, look at the bike price alone, and check what the membership renews into. It is worth something only if you would have paid for that app anyway.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a closed bike for the hardware price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A big discount on a bike tied to one platform still leaves the membership to pay for as long as you ride it, and some bikes lose features without it. Add the membership over three years before comparing it with an open bike like the IC4.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cheap original Peloton without checking the seat post</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The original Peloton Bike (PL01) and Bike+ (PL02 with a "T" serial) have both been recalled for seat posts that can break. If one is offered cheaply second-hand or by a marketplace seller, check the model and serial number and confirm the free replacement seat post has been fitted before anyone rides it.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

